WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.847 --> 00:00:05.270
ITIL is the best-known collection of good practice for IT service management:

2
00:00:05.421 --> 00:00:08.672
organising IT so that users can work reliably.

3
00:00:09.653 --> 00:00:13.380
It began in the late nineteen eighties at the British government.

4
00:00:14.152 --> 00:00:15.139
The current version,

5
00:00:15.302 --> 00:00:20.468
ITIL four, appeared in twenty nineteen and is now owned by the company PeopleCert.

6
00:00:21.517 --> 00:00:23.456
You do not need to know it by heart.

7
00:00:24.234 --> 00:00:28.924
But you must keep three words sharply apart: incident, problem and change.

8
00:00:30.251 --> 00:00:33.351
An incident is an unplanned interruption of a service,

9
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.685
or a service working worse than it should.

10
00:00:36.458 --> 00:00:40.337
The MES does not respond, the label printer does not print.

11
00:00:41.377 --> 00:00:44.872
The goal with an incident: working again as fast as possible,

12
00:00:45.035 --> 00:00:47.067
even if you do not know the cause yet.

13
00:00:48.163 --> 00:00:50.183
Incidents come in at the service desk:

14
00:00:50.322 --> 00:00:54.130
the central point where users report outages and questions,

15
00:00:54.304 --> 00:00:56.243
by phone, email or portal.

16
00:00:57.575 --> 00:01:02.510
A problem in ITIL is the cause, or potential cause, of one or more incidents.

17
00:01:03.515 --> 00:01:07.242
With a problem the goal is different: understanding why it happens,

18
00:01:07.486 --> 00:01:08.995
so it does not come back.

19
00:01:10.005 --> 00:01:14.069
The printer that fails every week: that is fifty-two incidents a year,

20
00:01:14.185 --> 00:01:15.880
but maybe only one problem,

21
00:01:16.066 --> 00:01:19.781
like a power supply that dips briefly when the press starts up.

22
00:01:20.508 --> 00:01:22.760
Problem management looks for that cause.

23
00:01:24.116 --> 00:01:25.149
A change is adding,

24
00:01:25.265 --> 00:01:30.524
modifying or removing anything that could affect a service: a new MES version,

25
00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:33.950
a different printer driver, an adjusted firewall rule.

26
00:01:34.937 --> 00:01:37.596
Many incidents are caused by a change.

27
00:01:38.304 --> 00:01:42.751
That is why the first question at an outage is often: what changed recently?

28
00:01:43.739 --> 00:01:47.303
ITIL four now calls managing changes change enablement,

29
00:01:47.431 --> 00:01:51.611
at first change control: making changes possible, not stopping them.

30
00:01:52.886 --> 00:01:54.523
For you as a Lean consultant:

31
00:01:54.628 --> 00:01:58.587
handling an incident is like quickly fixing a breakdown on the line.

32
00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:03.917
Problem management is like a root-cause analysis, so it does not return.

33
00:02:04.621 --> 00:02:08.324
The difference is in the goal: working again, versus never again.

34
00:02:09.326 --> 00:02:13.064
What you often see: a service desk that is good at incidents,

35
00:02:13.181 --> 00:02:15.213
while nobody has time for problems.

36
00:02:16.248 --> 00:02:20.683
A sharp question: which five outages came back most often last month,

37
00:02:20.857 --> 00:02:22.529
and who is looking for the cause?

38
00:02:23.856 --> 00:02:28.430
An example: at a cooked-meats plant these reports come in at the service desk.

39
00:02:29.135 --> 00:02:30.435
This is how you sort them.

40
00:02:31.472 --> 00:02:36.940
Monday, ten past six: the scale on line two sends no weights to the MES.

41
00:02:37.658 --> 00:02:40.270
An incident: the service is interrupted.

42
00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.291
The service desk restarts the link, and at twenty-five past six it works again.

43
00:02:47.276 --> 00:02:52.489
Tuesday at five past six and Wednesday at twelve past six: the same report.

44
00:02:53.244 --> 00:02:54.591
Two more incidents.

45
00:02:55.621 --> 00:03:00.590
Wednesday afternoon the IT administrator sees the pattern: three incidents,

46
00:03:00.787 --> 00:03:02.134
always around six.

47
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:07.458
That becomes one problem: scale link line two fails at shift start.

48
00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.945
On Thursday the cause turns out to be a

49
00:03:11.015 --> 00:03:13.511
scheduled backup at six that blocks the link.

50
00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:18.781
Moving the backup to four is a change, which is first discussed and planned.
